      "Content credentials and signatures can preserve a capture and edit history, but they cannot prove that an authentic photograph has honest context. They can also disappear after a screenshot. Provenance standards are infrastructure, not universal lie detectors.",
      "Watermarks have similar limits. They can help platforms identify synthetic material and enforce policy, but compression, cropping, or regeneration may damage the signal. Robust systems combine credentials, watermarks, platform records, forensic analysis, and human investigation.",
      "Software budgets will shift toward assurance. Faster code generation increases the value of tests, review, dependency scanning, and runtime monitoring; faster contract drafting raises the value of permissions, versioning, and signatures. Some saved production cost moves into quality control.",
      "Professional value changes as well. Experts will curate, certify, review, and accept responsibility in addition to producing material. A trusted brand increasingly means “we will sign our name to this result” rather than “we can create it.”",
      "Verification must not become a toll that only large firms can afford. Open standards, inexpensive signing, reproducible tests, and public fact infrastructure can prevent a handful of certification providers from monopolizing trust. Anonymous speech and whistleblowing need protected exceptions.",
      "When generation approaches zero cost, traceability, reproducibility, and accountability become expensive. The premium feature of a future product may be less “generate again” and more “show the evidence, run the checks, and name the party responsible.”",
